---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteImage from '../components/SiteImage.astro';

const criteria = [
  { name: 'Debris detection', target: 'Recall ≥ 70% for 5–25 mm items and ≥ 90% for items 25 mm or larger; precision ≥ 0.80' },
  { name: 'Density accuracy', target: 'Mean absolute percentage error ≤ 15% against plots with a known density' },
  { name: 'Repeatability', target: 'Coefficient of variation ≤ 10% across five or more surveys of the same plot' },
  { name: 'Coverage and counting', target: 'Surveyed area within 5% of the measured area; ≥ 95% of items in overlapping images or lanes counted exactly once' },
  { name: 'Survey efficiency', target: 'At least 1 m²/min, with the count and density produced automatically when the survey ends' },
];
---
<Layout>
<main id="main-content">
      <section class="hero-section">
        <div class="hero-inner">
          <div>
            <h1 class="hero-title">A low-cost rover that measures how much trash is on a beach</h1>
            <p class="hero-subtitle">
              DuneBroom drives a stretch of shoreline in lanes while a downward-facing camera and an
              onboard detection model count litter as small as 5&nbsp;mm. When the survey ends, it reports
              trash density in items per square meter, consistently enough to show whether a beach is
              getting cleaner.
            </p>
            <div class="button-group">
              <a href="/system-logic" class="btn-primary">How a survey works</a>
              <a href="/technical-architecture" class="btn-secondary">Technical architecture</a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <figure class="hero-visual">
            <SiteImage
              src="/robot-new.jpg"
              alt="DuneBroom prototype: a tracked rover carrying a Raspberry Pi 5 and Arduino, with a downward-facing camera on a PVC boom reaching out ahead of the tracks"
              width={1600}
              height={1204}
              class="hero-image" style="width: 100%; height: auto"
              sizes="(max-width: 1023px) 100vw, 640px"
              loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"
            />
            <dl class="spec-strip">
              <div><dt>Smallest debris counted</dt><dd class="figure">5&nbsp;mm</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Density error target</dt><dd class="figure">&le;&nbsp;15%</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Run-to-run variation target</dt><dd class="figure">&le;&nbsp;10%</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Total hardware cost</dt><dd class="figure">&lt;&nbsp;$350</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="overview" class="overview-section">
        <div class="overview-content">
          <h2 class="text-[32px] leading-tight font-medium tracking-tight text-foreground mb-12 mt-0">
            Overview
          </h2>

          <div class="pb-10 mb-10 border-b border-border">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-medium text-foreground mb-4 mt-0">The problem</h3>
            <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-subtle mb-4">
              Communities spend a lot of effort cleaning beaches and keeping trash out of waterways. Finding
              out whether that effort works means measuring how much trash is on a shoreline and how that
              changes over time. Today that measurement is mostly done by people walking a plot and counting
              by hand.
            </p>
            <ul class="text-base leading-relaxed text-subtle mb-0 pl-5 space-y-2">
              <li>
                <strong class="font-medium text-foreground">Manual surveys take a lot of labor</strong>, which limits
                how often and how widely shorelines can be monitored.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong class="font-medium text-foreground">Results vary between surveys.</strong> How much debris
                surveyors find depends on the number of observers, the size and color of the debris, and the
                type of shoreline (Burgess et&nbsp;al., 2024).
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong class="font-medium text-foreground">Small pieces aren't counted.</strong> NOAA&apos;s standard
                shoreline protocol only counts debris 2.5&nbsp;cm or larger.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong class="font-medium text-foreground">Drones miss small items.</strong> In California field
                tests, aerial imagery found less debris than people counting on the ground (Moore et&nbsp;al., 2021).
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="pb-10 mb-10 border-b border-border">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-medium text-foreground mb-4 mt-0">The goal</h3>
            <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-subtle mb-0">
              Design and validate a camera-equipped ground rover that environmental researchers and municipal
              agencies could use to measure shoreline trash density accurately and repeatably, without counting
              by hand. The rover counts at ground level, so it can see items much smaller than the 2.5&nbsp;cm
              cutoff. It is tested against plots where the exact number, size and position of every item is known.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="pb-10 mb-10 border-b border-border">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-medium text-foreground mb-2 mt-0">Design criteria</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted mb-6 mt-0">These are the targets the rover is being tested against. Testing is in progress.</p>
            <dl class="m-0">
              {criteria.map(({ name, target }) => (
                <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-[200px_1fr] gap-1 sm:gap-6 py-4 border-t border-border first:border-t-0 first:pt-0">
                  <dt class="text-base font-medium text-foreground">{name}</dt>
                  <dd class="text-base leading-relaxed text-subtle m-0">{target}</dd>
                </div>
              ))}
            </dl>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-medium text-foreground mb-6 mt-0">Key capabilities</h3>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-5">
              <div class="bg-surface p-5 rounded-xl border border-border">
                <h4 class="text-base font-medium text-foreground mb-2 mt-0">
                  Small-debris detection
                </h4>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed mb-0">
                  A YOLOv8 model fine-tuned on shoreline images separates litter from shells, rocks and
                  seaweed, down to 5&nbsp;mm.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-surface p-5 rounded-xl border border-border">
                <h4 class="text-base font-medium text-foreground mb-2 mt-0">
                  Each item counted once
                </h4>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed mb-0">
                  Phase-correlation ground tracking and lane bands prevent double counting between
                  overlapping images and neighboring lanes.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-surface p-5 rounded-xl border border-border">
                <h4 class="text-base font-medium text-foreground mb-2 mt-0">
                  Fully onboard
                </h4>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed mb-0">
                  Capture, detection, counting and density calculation all run on a Raspberry Pi 5, with no
                  internet connection or external computer.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="evolution" class="overview-section">
        <div class="overview-content">
          <h2 class="text-[32px] leading-tight font-medium tracking-tight text-foreground mb-6 mt-0">
            From collecting trash to measuring it
          </h2>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-subtle mb-4">
            The first version of DuneBroom was built to pick trash up. It used a machine-learning model to tell
            litter from natural objects and a scooper to collect what it found. In practice, collection was
            unreliable: the rover often dropped debris, and the approach didn&apos;t scale.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-subtle mb-0">
            Working on it showed a more basic gap. Without accurate, consistent measurement, communities
            can&apos;t tell whether cleanup is reducing trash at all. After reviewing California&apos;s trash
            monitoring methods and talking with researchers at the San Francisco Estuary Institute, the
            project was redefined around measurement. The current rover is a new design. Apart from the
            tracked chassis, its camera system, detection model, counting algorithms and validation plan are
            all built from scratch.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="recognition-section">
        <div class="recognition-content">
          <h2 class="recognition-title">Recognition</h2>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-subtle max-w-xl mx-auto mb-4">
            The earlier, trash-collecting version of DuneBroom was named a 2025–2026{" "}
            <strong class="font-medium text-foreground">Conrad Challenge Innovator</strong>.
          </p>
          <a
            href="https://conrad.spacecenter.org/what-is-a-conrad-innovator/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="inline-block text-accent font-medium text-[15px] underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
          >
            Learn about Conrad Innovators &rarr;
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
</Layout>
